index 9fceea9..20e04b9 100644
--- a/docs/ARCHITECTURE.md
+++ b/docs/ARCHITECTURE.md
@@ -71,7 +71,7 @@ The client is made of plain script files in the `public/` folder. There is no bu
 
 The interface uses the Frequency layout: a top bar (the frequency info and the Online strip on the left, Search in the exact center, and the Direct messages, Friends and profile buttons on the right), the frequency band where the joined frequencies (each a separate Telsiz server) are arranged as stations (`24-frekans.js`, pressing a station switches to that frequency), the conversation column in the middle, and on wide screens Notifications with the radio card at the very bottom of the column on the left and the list of text and voice rooms of the open frequency (Stations, with the people in each voice room under it, a sheet opened from the Stations button on narrow screens) on the right. Notifications (`28-bildirim.js`) is a short event list kept only in the client's memory and adds no new data on the server: joining and leaving voice rooms come from comparing the voice rosters (`meta.voice`) with the previous ones on every meta update, while a screen share is written from the encrypted share announcement that arrives over the voice connections, so only shares in the same voice room appear. The people in the Stations list are drawn from the same rosters, and the ring around the person speaking is known only in the room you are connected to. In the desktop app hidden background windows count the unread messages of frequencies that are not open (`25-arka-plan.js`, [desktop/README.en.md](../desktop/README.en.md)). Details are in [DESIGN.md](DESIGN.md). The service worker (`sw.js`) caches only the app shell and never touches `/api/` requests.
 
-Moving between conversations lives in `38-gezinme.js`. Room, direct message and Friends switches add an entry to the browser history, and the address and its `#` part never change (that part can carry a key or an invite). An entry carries only the user id, the distance from the root, the view type and a numeric id (`{ telsiz: { v: 1, u, d, view, id } }`). Sheets, the search panel, settings, the image viewer and the profile card add their own entry at every width (`{ telsiz: { v: 1, u, d, overlay } }`), and the entry is taken back when the layer closes some other way. The back button first closes the open layer, then returns to the previous conversation. When a layer without its own entry (a menu, the emoji picker, the enlarged camera view, a confirmation dialog) is on top, back closes only that layer and moves the history back to where it was, so the conversation does not change. Pressing back on the root entry leaves the app through the browser, no trap entry is added. When the session ends (Log Out or a lost session) the history is rewound to the root entry even when no layer is open. The rewind amount is read from the distance stored in the browser's current entry, so it never goes past the root even when the in-memory model is stale. An entry from another account and a history event outside the app are ignored. Read marks (`telsiz.read.<user>`) are kept only on the device and are not synced between devices. A conversation counts as read only while the person is at the bottom and the newest messages are loaded, so a message that arrives while they are scrolled up is not counted as read. The Mark as Read button reads the whole conversation and asks the server for the id of the newest message when the newest messages are not loaded. When a conversation opens and the first unread message is not on the newest page, the page is requested around the last read message (`around`) and newer messages load as the person scrolls down. This needed no server change. The open view (a text room, a direct conversation or Friends, `telsiz.view.<user>`) is stored on the device and restored when the page reloads.
+Moving between conversations lives in `38-gezinme.js`. Room, direct message and Friends switches add an entry to the browser history, and the address and its `#` part never change (that part can carry a key or an invite). An entry carries only the user id, the distance from the root, the view type and a numeric id (`{ telsiz: { v: 1, u, d, view, id } }`). Sheets, the search panel, settings, the image viewer and the profile card add their own entry at every width (`{ telsiz: { v: 1, u, d, overlay } }`), and the entry is taken back when the layer closes some other way. The back button first closes the open layer, then returns to the previous conversation. When a layer without its own entry (a menu, the emoji picker, the enlarged camera view, a confirmation dialog) is on top, back closes only that layer and moves the history back to where it was, so the conversation does not change. Pressing back on the root entry leaves the app through the browser, no trap entry is added. When the session ends (Log Out or a lost session) the history is rewound to the root entry even when no layer is open. The rewind amount is read from the distance stored in the browser's current entry, so it never goes past the root even when the in-memory model is stale. An entry from another account and a history event outside the app are ignored. Read marks (`telsiz.read.<user>`) are kept only on the device and are not synced between devices. A conversation counts as read only while the person is at the bottom and the newest messages are loaded, so a message that arrives while they are scrolled up is not counted as read. The Mark as Read button reads the whole conversation and asks the server for the id of the newest message when the newest messages are not loaded. When a conversation opens and the first unread message is not on the newest page, the page is requested around the last read message (`around`) and newer messages load as the person scrolls down. The unread count comes from both pages together, and it is a lower bound when the pages do not meet. If the conversation was not counted as read when it opened (the person is not at the bottom), this count and the mentions among those messages go into the counters. The startup scan (`scanUnread`, `scanDmUnread`) skips the conversation that opens at startup or after a reload, so this is what keeps its badge when the person moves to another conversation without scrolling down. This needed no server change. The open view (a text room, a direct conversation or Friends, `telsiz.view.<user>`) is stored on the device and restored when the page reloads.
 
 Data stored on the device lives in the browser's local storage: the session token, the group keyring (`telsiz.keys`), the personal identity key, pinned contact keys, theme and voice settings, the YouTube consent, read marks and the last open view. The search index exists only in the memory of the session and is never written to disk.
 
